      "versionNonce": 400368199,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1751977522308,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Solution Algorithms: \n\n1.  Random generate -> bad solution\n    high changes of collison. like with\n    set of values like a..z, A..Z etc.\n\n\n2. [ LIBRARY ] Generate some code with help of\n    timestamp, service name, instanceid\n    basically something already \n    generated by twitter/ sony etc. \n\n    \n   a. timestamp in millisecond. ( 64 )\n   b. instanceid  -> k8s pod name \n   c. serviceid    -> unique across org. \n   d. sequencenumber -> instance level numbering ( 1024 -> 10)\n   \ninstance id unique across instance.\nservice id across service\n\n\nlimitation -> atmax sequence number \nof unique ids in 1 miliseconds.\n\nif its 1024 -> 1M / second. \n\n3.  Generate sequence number with \n    mysql db or redis. \n\n    these will make sure to have atomic\n    id, only one will be allow to increment\n    id. \n\n   can have some latency issue in concurreny.\n   limitation: predictable.\n\n   if using redis , need to make it reliable, what if\n   its crashes, still might be possible not gauranteed \n   in very worst case\n  \n   workaround -> master replica, aof enable, at restart\n   increment start value what in replica + some large value\n\n\nREDIS -> INCR for key -> this is atomic in nature\n\nmaintain the master -> 2 replicate to increase availability\nand also enable aof to log key in log. \n\nMYSQL -> make the row with very less data. \n-> remove the old row. \n-> can be problematic in case of very high no. of rows. \n\nif 1M/s -> 1 day -> 10B rows. \n-> let say hour job -> 3.6 B\n\n\nCREATE TABLE atomic_counter (\n    id BIGINT NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n    PRIMARY KEY (id)\n);\n\nfunc getNextID(db *sql.DB) (int64, error) {\n    res, err := db.Exec(\"INSERT INTO atomic_counter () VALUES ()\")\n    if err != nil {\n        return 0, err\n    }\n    return res.LastInsertId()\n}\n\n\n\n4.  mantain ranges in highly consistency k:v like\n    zookeeper -> that will make sure about concurrency\n    and consistency , also have relatively less latency\n\n  limitation: if other service restart -> that range might \n  loose. \n\n  1000_1100 : true/false \n  1101_1200 : true/false\n\nfill the zookeerper with ranges of around 1 weeks\nand maintain queue to fill it continuous. \nand remove old. \n\n\n\n\n\n   \n\n    \n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Solution Algorithms: \n\n1.  Random generate -> bad solution\n    high changes of collison. like with\n    set of values like a..z, A..Z etc.\n\n\n2. [ LIBRARY ] Generate some code with help of\n    timestamp, service name, instanceid\n    basically something already \n    generated by twitter/ sony etc. \n\n    \n   a. timestamp in millisecond. ( 64 )\n   b. instanceid  -> k8s pod name \n   c. serviceid    -> unique across org. \n   d. sequencenumber -> instance level numbering ( 1024 -> 10)\n   \ninstance id unique across instance.\nservice id across service\n\n\nlimitation -> atmax sequence number \nof unique ids in 1 miliseconds.\n\nif its 1024 -> 1M / second. \n\n3.  Generate sequence number with \n    mysql db or redis. \n\n    these will make sure to have atomic\n    id, only one will be allow to increment\n    id. \n\n   can have some latency issue in concurreny.\n   limitation: predictable.\n\n   if using redis , need to make it reliable, what if\n   its crashes, still might be possible not gauranteed \n   in very worst case\n  \n   workaround -> master replica, aof enable, at restart\n   increment start value what in replica + some large value\n\n\nREDIS -> INCR for key -> this is atomic in nature\n\nmaintain the master -> 2 replicate to increase availability\nand also enable aof to log key in log. \n\nMYSQL -> make the row with very less data. \n-> remove the old row. \n-> can be problematic in case of very high no. of rows. \n\nif 1M/s -> 1 day -> 10B rows. \n-> let say hour job -> 3.6 B\n\n\nCREATE TABLE atomic_counter (\n    id BIGINT NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n    PRIMARY KEY (id)\n);\n\nfunc getNextID(db *sql.DB) (int64, error) {\n    res, err := db.Exec(\"INSERT INTO atomic_counter () VALUES ()\")\n    if err != nil {\n        return 0, err\n    }\n    return res.LastInsertId()\n}\n\n\n\n4.  mantain ranges in highly consistency k:v like\n    zookeeper -> that will make sure about concurrency\n    and consistency , also have relatively less latency\n\n  limitation: if other service restart -> that range might \n  loose. \n\n  1000_1100 : true/false \n  1101_1200 : true/false\n\nfill the zookeerper with ranges of around 1 weeks\nand maintain queue to fill it continuous. \nand remove old. \n\n\n\n\n\n   \n\n    \n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
